\section{Data and Variables}

%\textbf{Data Preparation Steps}


 All data used in this thesis  has been taken from the following public sources:
 
\begin{itemize}[topsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,itemsep=0.5 pt,parsep= 0.5 pt]
      
      \item \textit{EBA Transparency Exercise: Databases published between 2015  and  2020} \\*
      \textcolor{blue}{\url{https://eba.europa.eu/risk-analysis-and-data/eu-wide-transparency-exercise}} 
      \item \textit{IMF Data}\\*
      \textcolor{blue}{\url{https://data.imf.org}}
      \item \textit{World Bank Data}\\*
      \textcolor{blue}{\url{https://datacatalog.worldbank.org/}}
      \item \textit{World Bank Doing Business Report}\\*
      % The Doing Business project provides objective measures of business regulations and 
      % their enforcement as recovery rates or resolving insolvencies\\*
      \textcolor{blue}{\url{https://www.doingbusiness.org/}}
      
      
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Data Preparation}

\renewcommand{\labelitemi}{$\ast$}
\renewcommand{\labelitemii}{$\diamond$}
\renewcommand{\labelitemiii}{$\cdot$}
\renewcommand{\labelitemiv}{$ $}
%\renewcommand{\labelitemi}{$\bullet$}

%\begin{itemize}

%\item  \textit{Bank-specific data:}


As  mentioned in previous sections, an extensive, granular dataset on major European banks from the EBA transparency exercises  published between 2015 and 2020 will be used in this thesis along with World Bank and IMF data for the macroeconomic information. The (unbalanced) sample consists of over 130 banks, at the highest level of consolidation, from 27 countries in the EU and the European Economic Area (EEA)  on an annual basis between 2014 and 2019. This dataset is very rich in dimensionality in terms of banks, exposure types, geographic allocation of exposures and performing status among others. 

Once the full databases from  the EBA's EU-wide Transparency Exercise were downloaded for every annual exercise and  template class (credit risk -cre-, market risk -mrk-, sovereign exposures -sov-, other templates-oth-), an intensive data preparation process (collecting, cleaning, and consolidation) has been exercised to aggregate the data in .csv files provided by the EBA website for each year into an aggregated database. These .csv files are split by credit risk (tre\_cre.csv), market risk (tre\_mrk.csv), sovereign exposures (tre\_sov.csv) and  other templates (tre\_oth.csv), covering all available data from the transparency exercises that took place between 2015 and 2020.

 In the same way, metadata and mapping files were also downloaded for each EBA transparency exercise that took place between 2015 and 2020. These files were used to:
 
 \begin{itemize}[topsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,itemsep=0.5 pt,parsep= 0.5 pt]

 \item Identify mapping rules for the different data dimensions (performing status, exposure type, portfolio class, etc...)
 
 \item Merge data from different exercises for each bank  and item  in order to identify common LEI-codes and item numbers (same LEI code for each bank and same item number for each item for the total period 2015-2020). This is necessary because these identifiers and the specific banks participating in each exercise may change from year to year. 
 %Finally, the concatenation of data from different yearly exercises into a %common database was carried out.
\end{itemize}


For the disaggregation in exposure types, the following item is provided by the EBA:

 \begin{itemize} [label=$\cdot$,topsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,itemsep=0.5 pt,parsep= 0.5 pt]
 \item \textbf{\textit{Item Name:}} Exposure type [exposure]
 \item  \textbf{\textit{Definition}}: Dimensions of exposure types:
 
    % \begin{itemize}[label= $-$,topsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,itemsep=0.5 pt,parsep= 0.5 pt]
    %     \item Central banks
    %     \item Credit institutions
    %     \item General governments
    %     \item Financial corporations other than credit institutions
    %     \item Households
    %     \item Non-financial corporations
    % \end{itemize}
 \item  \textbf{\textit{Source}}: EBA transparency exercises Databases 
 \par
\begingroup
\leftskip1em
\rightskip\leftskip
 \textit{Group}: cre (Credit Risk and Asset Quality)\\
\textit{Sheet}:  NPE\\
\textit{Dimensions (exposure) from EBA 2020}:
\begin{itemize}[topsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,itemsep=0.5 pt,parsep= 0.5 pt] 
        \item 101=Central banks
        \item 102=Credit institutions
        \item 201=General governments
        \item 202=Financial corporations other than credit institutions
        \item 301=Households
        \item 401=Non-financial corporations
    \end{itemize}

\par
\endgroup
 
  \end{itemize}
    

However, only the following exposure types provided NPL data:

	\begin{itemize} [label= $-$,topsep=0pt, partopsep=0pt,itemsep=0.5 pt,parsep= 0.5 pt]
      	
      	\item Households
      	\item Financial corporations other than credit institutions
      	\item Non-Financial Institutions
      	
    \end{itemize}

The rest of available exposure types (Central Banks, Credit Institutions, General Governments) do not provide NPL data in most cases and, therefore, these types  will not be included in the analysis. Furthermore, their relevance to understand  the determinants of NPL is  usually secondary compared to the previous ones. In other words, the disaggregated analysis in this thesis will focus on households, non-financial institutions and financial corporations other than credit institutions.

The selection of the bank-specific variables  was based on using the most common variables  analyzed in the NPL literature as long as these were also available in the EBA Transparency Exercise database for the period 2014-2019. The selection of macroeconomic variables were also based on taking variables commonly examined in NPL analyses. In order to obtain some bank-specific  indicators the application of  simple calculations  using individual items from the EBA transparency exercises was also needed due to the fact that the values of some relevant financial ratios were not directly included in the databases provided by the EBA. These calculations have been validated by comparing the final values at country level with those provided by the EBA in several publicly available reports and dashboards, which also used the EBA transparency exercise databases for analyses at country-level, specifically, the NPL Report 2019 (\cite{EBA}), key statistics for EBA's EU-wide Transparency Exercise spring-2020 (\cite{EBA3}) and Risk Dashboard for Q4 2019 (\cite{EBA2}). A detailed description of the formulas applied are also available in the next sections.

As leverage exposures were not provided for  the EBA Transparency Exercise of 2016, the generated missing values in December 2015 for some bank-specific variables  that needed leverage data for the calculation were linearly extrapolated using all available data. In order to obtain a valid extrapolation, all banks with less than four periods of available data (semesterly or quarterly) were excluded from the sample. In other words, linear imputation methods are applied over these missing values for December 2015. Additionally, observations with missing non-performing loan ratios or non-plausible values in any of the regressors were also excluded from the sample.

